  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose to solve the problem using a reinforcement learning approach. The central idea is to represent the optimization as a path finding problem on a decision diagram. The function h(x) is represented as a binary sequence (step function) with n samples. The objective is to minimize max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx. \n\nThe solution search space is partitioned into phases (resolutions), where at each phase:\n- We consider a fixed grid resolution n_points (which increases across phases)\n- We decompose the correlation function into layers of fixed overlap scenarios\n- We use a reinforcement agent that learns to maintain low overlap values through trial and selection\n\nKey differentiators from the parent approach:\n- Explicitly tracks the layered structure of overlaps\n- preserves feasibility at multi-resolution scales\n- Combines policy gradient with progressive resolution increases\n- Includes automatic correlation-based weighting of solution dimensions\n\nThis approach differs significantly from gradient-based or local search methods. The mathematical structure exploits the fact that the optimal solution must balance between minimal correlation at small overlaps and controlled decoupling at large overlaps.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- Binary representation h_values \u2208 {0,1}^{n_points}\n- Overlap layers L_k for k=0,1,...,K (K=2*n_points-1)\n- Layer weights w_k that model the contribution of different overlap scenarios \n- Reinforcement policy \u03c0_\u03b8(a|s) where \u03b8 is the neural network parameter\n\nObjective:\nMinimize J = max_k [ (1/2/n_points) * sum_k w_k * corr_k ] \nsubject to:\n- np.sum(h_values) = n_points/2\n- Tied node representation consistent across resolutions\n\nThe optimization is performed through:\n1. Layered value estimation\n2. Deep policy-based reinforcement learning\n3. Progressive resolution transitions\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep 1: Problem validation\n- Check input resolution n_start\n- Verify initial conditions using provided initial_h_values if available\n\nStep 2: Algorithm setup\n- Initialize policy gradient agent (Dense layers)\n- Parameterize layer weights using problem decomposition\n- Set up multi-resolution staging\n\nStep 3: Core loop\nFor T in range(max_iterations):\n    - Maintain historical best solutions across resolutions\n    - Perform episodic selection and refinement\n    - Layer-wise correlation calculation\n    - Update policy network\n    - Manage resolution transitions (128\u2192256\u2192512\u21921024)\n\nStep 4: Feasibility and repair\n- Adjust solution distribution to enforce \u2211h_values=1/2 \n- Use automatic projection weights to scale resolutions\n- Only valid step functions (\"clean\" H-value transitions) propagate\n\nStep 5: Reward shaping\n- Primary reward: -J (layered objective)\n- Secondary reward: structural consistency score\n- Action shaping reward based on gradient alignment\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_start = 128 (initial resolution)\n- Max_iterations = 3000\n- Resolution doublings: 128, 256, 512, 1024\n- Policy layers: [128,64,32] for action-value estimation\n- Exploration rate: 0.3 (decaying polynomial)\n- Correlation-based weighting factor: \u03bb=0.01\n\nStochastic component adjustment triggers:\n- Plateau detection (5 consecutive worse values per resolution)\n- Cross-entropy rate MLE\n- Dimension correlation threshold > 0.7\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Primary eval time per resolution: T(n) \u221d n log n\n- Phase allocation:\n  Phase 1 (128): 10%\n  Phase 2 (256): 30%\n  Phase 3 (512): 40%\n  Phase 4 (1024): 20%\n- Resource monitoring:\n  - Check time per phase (progressive warmup)\n  - Failure recovery at phase boundaries (downgrade resolution)\n  - Early termination if plateau detected (>10% worse than best)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Best solution final projection to validation set\n- Re-compute C5 bound: compute_c5(best_h, n_final)\n- Final checks:\n  - 0 \u2264 h_values \u2264 1 for all positions\n  - |sum(h_values) - n/2| < 1e-5\n  - Finite, non-zero score\n  - Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
